From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 00:40:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] net: ipa: tell the modem where its hashed tables are

An LTE attach on this device hangs the modem hard -- hard enough that it
never records a reason -- and its Q6 watchdog takes the whole SoC down about
ten seconds later. GPRS on the same hardware carries real traffic with no
errors.

init_modem_driver_req() fills in where the modem's header, route, filter and
processing context tables live. It does not fill in the hashed ones, and the
message has had the fields and the TLV encoding for them all along:

	v4_hash_route_tbl_info		tlv 0x1b
	v6_hash_route_tbl_info		tlv 0x1c
	v4_hash_filter_tbl_start
	v6_hash_filter_tbl_start
	hw_stats_quota_base_addr
	hw_stats_quota_size

They are not vestigial. ipa_table_hash_support() is true for everything except
v4.2, the layout allocates all four hashed tables, and ipa_table_init() sets
them up. Downstream sends every one of these, guarded by the same condition,
and the property that would disable hashing, qcom,ipa-fltrt-not-hashable, is
not set on this SoC -- it appears only in monaco.dtsi.

So the modem is told where its unhashed tables are and left to guess about the
hashed ones. A modem that installs a hashed rule against an address it was
never given writes wherever the unset field leaves it, and offset zero in
IPA's local memory is the microcontroller's shared area. That would explain a
hang with no assert: the modem waits on a microcontroller that is no longer
answering.

It also explains why GPRS is fine. At that rate the modem has no reason to
populate a hashed table; an LTE bearer does.

Fill them in, from the same layout the unhashed ones come from.
---
--- a/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_qmi.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_qmi.c
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 #include "ipa_mem.h"
 #include "ipa_modem.h"
 #include "ipa_qmi_msg.h"
+#include "ipa_table.h"
 
 /**
  * DOC: AP/Modem QMI Handshake
@@ -344,6 +345,46 @@
 
 	/* Nothing to report for the compression table (zip_tbl_info) */
 
+	/* The hashed tables are in the layout and the hardware supports them
+	 * everywhere except v4.2, but nothing here has ever told the modem
+	 * where they are. Downstream does, under the same condition, and a
+	 * modem that installs a hashed rule against an address it was never
+	 * given writes somewhere it should not -- offset zero in IPA's local
+	 * memory is the microcontroller's shared area.
+	 */
+	if (ipa_table_hash_support(ipa)) {
+		mem = ipa_mem_find(ipa, IPA_MEM_V4_ROUTE_HASHED);
+		req.v4_hash_route_tbl_info_valid = 1;
+		req.v4_hash_route_tbl_info.start =
+			ipa->mem_offset + mem->offset;
+		req.v4_hash_route_tbl_info.end = modem_route_count - 1;
+
+		mem = ipa_mem_find(ipa, IPA_MEM_V6_ROUTE_HASHED);
+		req.v6_hash_route_tbl_info_valid = 1;
+		req.v6_hash_route_tbl_info.start =
+			ipa->mem_offset + mem->offset;
+		req.v6_hash_route_tbl_info.end = modem_route_count - 1;
+
+		mem = ipa_mem_find(ipa, IPA_MEM_V4_FILTER_HASHED);
+		req.v4_hash_filter_tbl_start_valid = 1;
+		req.v4_hash_filter_tbl_start = ipa->mem_offset + mem->offset;
+
+		mem = ipa_mem_find(ipa, IPA_MEM_V6_FILTER_HASHED);
+		req.v6_hash_filter_tbl_start_valid = 1;
+		req.v6_hash_filter_tbl_start = ipa->mem_offset + mem->offset;
+	}
+
+	/* The modem's quota statistics area, which downstream reports
+	 * unconditionally. It does not report the drop area, so neither do we.
+	 */
+	mem = ipa_mem_find(ipa, IPA_MEM_STATS_QUOTA_MODEM);
+	if (mem && mem->size) {
+		req.hw_stats_quota_base_addr_valid = 1;
+		req.hw_stats_quota_base_addr = ipa->mem_offset + mem->offset;
+		req.hw_stats_quota_size_valid = 1;
+		req.hw_stats_quota_size = mem->size;
+	}
+
 	mem = ipa_mem_find(ipa, IPA_MEM_V4_ROUTE_HASHED);
 	if (mem->size) {
 		req.v4_hash_route_tbl_info_valid = 1;
